//
// PROBE: navigating the stack ring.
//
// The stacks form a deque and the **current stack is its back**
// (reference/rust_multistack/src/ts_current.rs:7), so navigation is rotation
// of that ring. `stacks_right` is `ts.right()` and pushes nothing
// (reference/rust_multistack/src/stdlib/rotate.rs:13-19) — a *move*, not a
// count, which the arrow alias `->` makes plainer than the name does.
//
// **`stacks_left` is absent on purpose — F71.** It is registered only as a
// function and never inline (`rotate.rs:93`), so the bare word is unreachable
// in the reference and so are its aliases `<-` and `←`. Bund2 registers it as
// every neighbour in that file is registered, so it works here; a golden
// cannot compare the two, because one side errors.
//
:a ensure_stack
:b ensure_stack
@main
"probe: stacks_right moves around the ring" println
current println
stacks_right
current println
"probe: the arrow alias is the same word" println
->
current println
"probe: stack_exists answers for present and absent names" println
:a stack_exists println
:nosuch stack_exists println
// `ensure_stack_with_capacity` takes the **name on top** and the capacity
// beneath (`ensure_stack.rs:36,42`), and the capacity is real: a push onto a
// full stack drops the value **most recently pushed** before adding the new
// one (`ts_push.rs:52-57`), so a stack capped at 2 given `1 2 3 4` keeps `1`
// and `4`. Not what "capacity" usually means, and preserved as written.
"probe: a capacity bounds the stack, discarding the newest" println
2 :capped ensure_stack_with_capacity
@capped
1 2 3 4
debug.display_stack
@main
